Stop an idle 8254 counter in mode 4/5 from re-arming its update timer forever.

UPSTREAM DEFECT, not an IRIX or SGI one. This patch is written to be sent to
mamedev as-is: it touches only src/devices/machine/pit8253.cpp, it adds one
line, and it makes modes 4 and 5 behave the way modes 0 and 1 already do.

WHAT IS WRONG
-------------
`pit_counter_device::simulate()` ends every mode by choosing how many input
clocks remain until the next OUTPUT transition, in `cycles_to_output`, which is
initialised to 0 and only assigned inside a per-mode `switch (m_phase)`.  The
caller treats 0 as "the output changes right now":

	if (cycles_to_output == CYCLES_NEVER || m_clockin == 0)
		adjust_timer(attotime::never);
	else
		adjust_timer(m_last_updated + cycles_to_output * m_clock_period);

Modes 4 and 5 (software- and hardware-triggered strobe) reach phase 0 in normal
operation: after the one-clock strobe, `simulate()` sets `m_phase = 0`, raises
the output, and the counter free-runs with the output HIGH until the guest
writes a new count.  MAME's own phase table in that file documents it —

	    0|high  |infinity|0..1 |0   |waiting for count/counting down

— "infinity", next phase 0.  But the mode 4/5 `switch (m_phase)` has cases 1, 2
and 3 only.  Phase 0 falls through it with `cycles_to_output` still 0, so the
update timer is armed at `m_last_updated + 0`, i.e. immediately.  It fires,
`update()` computes `elapsed_cycles == 0` and takes its other arm,
`adjust_timer(m_last_updated + m_clock_period)`, which fires one clock later and
lands back in `simulate()` in phase 0.  The counter therefore re-arms TWICE PER
INPUT CLOCK, forever, and not one of those re-arms ever changes the output or
raises an interrupt.

Modes 0 and 1 do not have the bug: mode 0 tests `m_phase == 0` explicitly and
mode 1's switch ends in `default: cycles_to_output = CYCLES_NEVER`.  Modes 2 and
3 cannot reach phase 0 inside the branch that runs the switch — they handle it
in the preceding `if (m_gate == 0 || m_phase == 0)` arm — so modes 4 and 5 are
the whole of the defect.  The fix is mode 1's idiom, applied to modes 4/5.

HOW IT SHOWS UP
---------------
Any driver whose guest leaves an 8253/8254 counter in mode 4 or 5 without a
count pays for it in host CPU forever.  On MAME's `indy_4610` (SGI Indy) it is
the dominant timer event in the machine: IRIX 6.5 programs IOC2 counter 2 to
mode 4 about two emulated seconds into the boot (control byte 0xb8) and never
takes it out, and from then on the counter re-arms

    2,000,000 times per emulated second   (1 MHz counter clock, twice per clock)

measured directly with a per-(counter, mode, phase) histogram in adjust_timer().
That is 90.4% of all timer events the machine raises.  With this patch the same
counter contributes 74 update_tick calls per 20 emulated seconds.

CAUTION FOR ANY DRIVER ADOPTING THIS
------------------------------------
The storm is expensive, but on a driver that sets no scheduling quantum of its
own it is also doing a job: MAME breaks a timeslice at the next timer expiry, so
a timer firing every half microsecond is what interleaves the CPU with the rest
of the machine.  Removing it raises `indy_4610`'s cycles per `execute_run()`
from 88.9 to ~673 and lets the CPU run to MAME's default 1/60 s quantum.  On
this driver that is paired with an explicit `set_maximum_quantum()`; see
mame-indy-scheduling-quantum.patch.  Guest-visible timing is unaffected either
way — the removed re-arms never drove an output edge, and the counter's VALUE is
still brought up to date lazily by `update()` on every read, gate change and
control write.

diff --git a/src/devices/machine/pit8253.cpp b/src/devices/machine/pit8253.cpp
--- a/src/devices/machine/pit8253.cpp
+++ b/src/devices/machine/pit8253.cpp
@@ -705,12 +705,13 @@

 			switch (m_phase)
 			{
 			case 1:  cycles_to_output = 1; break;
 			case 2:  cycles_to_output = adjusted_count(); break;
 			case 3:  cycles_to_output = 1; break;
+			default: cycles_to_output = CYCLES_NEVER; break;
 			}
 		}
 		break;
 	}

 	if (cycles_to_output == CYCLES_NEVER || m_clockin == 0)
